p_{\gamma}=p_{\text {recoil }}
\end{array}
\tag{36}
$$
Gamma-ray and recoiled nucleus have, of course, opposite directions. For gamma-ray (photon), energy and momentum are related as:
$$
E_{\gamma}=p_{\gamma} \cdot c
\tag{37}
$$
In part a) we have seen that the nucleus motion in this energy range is not relativistic, so we have:
$$
E_{\text {recoil }}=\frac{p_{\text {recii }}^{2}}{2 m\left({ }^{58} \mathrm{Ni}\right)}=\frac{p_{\gamma}^{2}}{2 m\left({ }^{58} \mathrm{Ni}\right)}=\frac{E_{\gamma}^{2}}{2 m\left({ }^{(58} \mathrm{Ni}\right) \cdot c^{2}}
\tag{38}
$$
Inserting this into law of energy conservation Eq. (36), we get:
$$
E_{x}\left({ }^{58} \mathrm{Ni}\right)=E_{\gamma}+E_{\text {recoil }}=E_{\gamma}+\frac{E_{\gamma}^{2}}{2 m\left({ }^{58} \mathrm{Ni}\right) \cdot c^{2}}
\tag{39}
$$
This reduces to the quadratic equation:
$$
E^2_{\gamma}+2m({}^{58}Ni)c^2\cdot E_{\gamma}+2m({}^{58}Ni)c^2 E_x({}^{58}Ni)=0
\tag{40}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
E_{\gamma} &= \frac{-2m({}^{58}Ni)c^2+\sqrt{4(m({}^{58}Ni)c^2)^2+8m({}^{58}Ni)c^2E_x({}^{58}Ni)}}{2} \\
&=\sqrt{(m({}^{58}Ni)c^2)^2+2m({}^{58}Ni)c^2E_x({}^{58}Ni)}-m({}^{58}Ni)c^2
\end{aligned}
\tag{41}
$$
Inserting numbers gives:
$$
