\end{aligned}
$$

The position of the upper part of $\mathrm{CM}$ when it contracts to a length $L_{0}-l$ is $H_{c m-u p p e r-f}=$ $\frac{l^{2}}{2 l_{0}}+\frac{l_{0}}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\left(L_{0}-l\right)$. The change in the CM during the contraction process is: $\Delta H_{c m-u p p e r}=$ $H_{\text {cm-upper }-i}-H_{\text {cm-upper }-f}=\frac{L_{0}^{2}+L_{0} l-2 l^{2}}{6 l_{0}}-\frac{1}{2}\left(L_{0}-l\right)=\frac{\left(L_{0}-l\right)\left(L_{0}+2 l\right)}{6 l_{0}}-\frac{1}{2}\left(L_{0}-l\right)$.

The acceleration of the $\mathrm{CM}$ of the upper part is $a_{C M}=\frac{F L_{0}}{M\left(L_{0}-l\right)}=\frac{g L_{0}}{L_{0}-l}$. From the work energy theorem we get the equation $v_{\text {upper-f }}^{2}=2 a_{C M} \Delta H_{\text {cm-upper }}$, hence

$$
\begin{gathered}
